                                           :elucidation "The class for materials categorised according to classical materials science, focusing on amorphous/crystalline nature, and main type categories (i.e., ceramic, composite, metallic and polymeric)."@en .


###  https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_3995e22d_5720_4dcf_ba3b_d0ce03f514c6
:EMMO_3995e22d_5720_4dcf_ba3b_d0ce03f514c6 rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                           rdfs:subClassOf :EMMO_6c487fb3_03d1_4e56_91ed_c2e16dcbef60 ,
                                                           :EMMO_a2b006f2_bbfd_4dba_bcaa_3fca20cd6be1 ;
                                           rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.britannica.com/science/gel> ;
                                           skos:prefLabel "Gel"@en ;
                                           :elucidation "A soft, solid or solid-like colloid consisting of two or more components, one of which is a liquid, present in substantial quantity."@en .


###  https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_40e18c93_a1b5_49ff_b06a_d9d932d1fb65
:EMMO_40e18c93_a1b5_49ff_b06a_d9d932d1fb65 rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                           rdfs:subClassOf :EMMO_6c487fb3_03d1_4e56_91ed_c2e16dcbef60 ,
                                                           :EMMO_7509da43_56b1_4d7f_887a_65d1663df4ba ;
                                           rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/E02065> ;
                                           skos:example "Mayonnaise"@en ,
                                                        "Milk"@en ;
                                           skos:prefLabel "Emulsion"@en ;
                                           :elucidation "An emulsion is a mixture of two or more liquids that are normally immiscible (a liquid-liquid heterogeneous mixture)."@en ;
                                           :wikipediaReference <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emulsion> .


###  https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_4207e895_8b83_4318_996a_72cfb32acd94
:EMMO_4207e895_8b83_4318_996a_72cfb32acd94 rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                           rdfs:subClassOf :EMMO_6e9cb807_fc68_4bcf_b3ba_5fccc887c644 ,
                                                           :EMMO_bc37743c_37c4_4ec7_9d58_d1aae5567352 ;
                                           rdfs:seeAlso isbn:9780080966656 ,
                                                        isbn:9780471736967 ;
                                           skos:prefLabel "Material"@en ;
                                           :conceptualisation "It is quite hard to define what a material is, especially in order to distinguish it from the more general matter concept. Here we decided first to restrict the domain from the bottom, by making a material always a substance, thus excluding all particulate matter (e.g., molecular entities such as atoms and molecules). Then, to call material every entity that can be categorised according to the typical criteria used in material science (e.g., by structure, function, size, isotropy). Such criteria usually categorise a substance according to kind, quality or quantity and are expressed in order to facilitate the identification of specific class of substances according to an intended use. In short, a material is what is called material in material science."@en ;
                                           :elucidation "A material is a substance categorized according to some criteria based on kind, quality or quantity. An intended use is typically associated with each of the classes used to categorize a material entity."@en ;
                                           :note "A instance of a material (e.g. nitrogen) can represent any state of matter. The fact that the individual also belongs to other classes (e.g. Gas) would reveal the actual form in which the material is found."@en ,
                                                 "A material may be either a mesoscopic or a continuum substance."@en .

[ rdf:type owl:Axiom ;
   owl:annotatedSource :EMMO_4207e895_8b83_4318_996a_72cfb32acd94 ;
   owl:annotatedProperty skos:prefLabel ;
   owl:annotatedTarget "Material"@en ;
   :etymology "From Latin māteria (“wood, material, substance”), from māter (“mother”) [Wikictionary]"@en
 ] .


