###  https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_a5da5097_9fb3_42c2_a6b2_b104f06ff773
:EMMO_a5da5097_9fb3_42c2_a6b2_b104f06ff773 rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                           rdfs:subClassOf :EMMO_4ec569e9_dfdb_4c75_81b6_381f2c54c585 ;
                                           skos:example "Borosilicate"@en ;
                                           skos:prefLabel "Glass"@en ;
                                           :conceptualisation "To be considered a true glass, an amorphous ceramic must have been formed via the glass transition (usually by cooling a liquid fast enough to freeze its chaotic structure in place)."@en ;
                                           :elucidation "An amorphous ceramic material obtained through a glass transition."@en ;
                                           :note "From a strict physics and thermodynamics standpoint, glass is a state of matter. Specifically, an amorphous network trapped in a structurally frozen state. Because this state is entirely dependent on temperature, glass can exist as a flowing, viscous liquid just as easily as it can exist as a brittle windowpane."@en ;
                                           :wikipediaReference <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glass> .


